// @emit-error field access receiver is not a struct
// A union ARM that is a struct carrying a STRUCT-TYPED field — the depth-2 shape — CONSTRUCTS
// and DISCRIMINATES, but the narrowed read of that field has no lowering. A VERDICT pin, not a
// log one: there is no correct output to assert, because the read does not emit at all.
//
// WHAT DOES WORK, MEASURED CELL BY CELL, because the gap is narrower than the message suggests:
//   - the arm's value boxes into the `{tag, value}` rep;
//   - `u is Outer` answers TRUE, and `u is i32` answers FALSE, for a member of this arm;
//   - a SCALAR sibling field of the same variant (`{k: i32, v: Inner}` → `u.k`) reads fine.
// Only the struct-typed field itself has no read: `structIndexOfExpr` classifies no receiver
// for it, so `u.v` — the ref, before `.v` is even applied — is what fails.
//
// THE SPELLING IS NOT THE AXIS, and this file uses the generic-free one to say so. The same
// shape written `Box<Box<i32>> | i32`, written inline as `{v: {v: i32}} | i32`, and written
// with the inner shape inline (`type Outer = { v: { v: i32 } }`) all stop in this same pass —
// three of them at this exact message. The generic spelling used to reach a SILENT wrong
// branch instead, but that was the `>>` close-credit fold collapsing the union before the
// emitter ever saw one (`generics/nested-application-union-arm.vl`) — a different defect, in
// a different pass, and fixing it is what lands the generic spelling here beside the rest.
type Inner = { v: i32 }
type Outer = { v: Inner }
type U = Outer | i32

const u: U = { v: { v: 5 } }
if u is Outer { print(u.v.v) } else { print(0) }
